== the bump ladder, on fixtures ==
ok  unchanged -> none
ok  regime added -> minor
ok  regime removed -> major
ok  payload schema changed -> major
ok  a cap moved -> patch
ok  the real penalty-schema/bump.yaml and rule.yaml parse with the standard library
ok  --tree refuses a declaration off the ladder rather than shrugging at it
ok  (1) a first release of v2 refuses v1's unreleased edit, naming the tag it drifted from
ok  (1) ...and admits it once v1 on disk is what v1.0.0 published
ok  (2) v1.1.0 declared minor over v1.0.0 is admitted
ok  (2) v1.0.1 declared minor is refused: the tag is a patch increment
ok  (2) --tree derives v1.1.0 from the declared minor and admits it
ok  (2) --tree refuses a declared none while the tree carries a minor
ok  (2) v2.0.0 declared minor is refused: the tag is a major increment
ok  (2) v1.0.1 declared none is refused: no tag carries none
ok  (3) a --no-tags clone of a released repository is refused, not graded as a first release
ok  (3) ...and --tree refuses the same way
ok  (3) a clone of a repository that never released takes the first-release path
ok  (3) ...and --tree names v1.0.0 as that first release

